00:09:55.315 --> 00:09:56.360
Key decisions.

169
00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:57.984
Here is a quick table:

170
00:09:58.264 --> 00:10:00.035
Decision, and Choice, and Why.

171
00:10:00.315 --> 00:10:01.317
Geography.

172
00:10:01.597 --> 00:10:02.429
Choice:

173
00:10:02.709 --> 00:10:09.579
City, then zones (hexagons, for example, H3, or polygons) for supply, demand and serviceability.

174
00:10:09.859 --> 00:10:10.541
Why:

175
00:10:10.821 --> 00:10:13.467
Bounded optimisation and local signals.

176
00:10:13.747 --> 00:10:14.856
Serviceability.

177
00:10:15.136 --> 00:10:15.968
Choice:

178
00:10:16.248 --> 00:10:21.795
Precomputed per (restaurant, customer hexagon) and refreshed every minute with zone stress.

179
00:10:22.075 --> 00:10:22.757
Why:

180
00:10:23.037 --> 00:10:24.723
Fast feed and honest ETAs.

181
00:10:25.003 --> 00:10:25.728
ETA.

182
00:10:26.008 --> 00:10:26.840
Choice:

183
00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:35.376
Decomposed: dispatch wait, rider to restaurant, prep remaining, pickup wait, travel to customer, last mile; ML per component.

184
00:10:35.656 --> 00:10:36.339
Why:

185
00:10:36.619 --> 00:10:38.155
Explainable, updatable.

186
00:10:38.435 --> 00:10:39.352
Dispatch.

187
00:10:39.632 --> 00:10:40.464
Choice:

188
00:10:40.744 --> 00:10:45.096
Batch optimisation every few seconds per zone, timed to food readiness.

189
00:10:45.376 --> 00:10:46.059
Why:

190
00:10:46.339 --> 00:10:48.472
Riders don't wait, food doesn't wait.

191
00:10:48.752 --> 00:10:49.669
Order state.

192
00:10:49.949 --> 00:10:50.781
Choice:

193
00:10:51.061 --> 00:10:53.429
Event-sourced state machine with Kafka events.

194
00:10:53.709 --> 00:10:54.392
Why:

195
00:10:54.672 --> 00:10:56.187
Many consumers, audit.

196
00:10:57.537 --> 00:10:58.561
Peak control.

197
00:10:58.841 --> 00:10:59.673
Choice:

198
00:10:59.953 --> 00:11:06.609
Zone-level levers: radius shrink, surge fees, longer ETAs, hiding far restaurants, rider incentives.

199
00:11:06.889 --> 00:11:07.571
Why:

200
00:11:07.851 --> 00:11:09.302
Keeps promises at peak.

201
00:11:09.582 --> 00:11:10.542
Location.

202
00:11:10.822 --> 00:11:11.654
Choice:

203
00:11:11.934 --> 00:11:15.881
In-memory geo index for live riders plus Kafka for history.

204
00:11:16.161 --> 00:11:16.843
Why:

205
00:11:17.123 --> 00:11:18.745
Fast nearest-rider queries.

206
00:11:20.095 --> 00:11:21.012
Part 4.

207
00:11:21.292 --> 00:11:23.169
How It Works, Journeys End to End.

208
00:11:24.419 --> 00:11:27.641
Journey 1: opening the app at 8:15 pm.

209
00:11:27.921 --> 00:11:28.881
Location.

210
00:11:29.161 --> 00:11:33.278
The app sends the customer's location (GPS or saved address).

211
00:11:33.558 --> 00:11:39.553
The server maps it to a hexagon (H3 resolution ~8 to 9) and to a delivery zone.

212
00:11:39.833 --> 00:11:42.307
Serviceability (§5.2).

213
00:11:42.587 --> 00:11:47.579
The serviceability service returns the restaurants that can deliver to this hexagon now.

214
00:11:47.859 --> 00:11:59.358
For each one it applies: open (timings, holiday, and whether it is currently accepting orders);. distance or travel time within the restaurant's radius, which shrinks when the zone is stressed;.

215
00:12:00.708 --> 00:12:13.807
ETA estimate: precomputed restaurant-to-hexagon travel time plus current prep-time and dispatch-delay signals;. delivery fee: base by distance, surge by zone stress, waived for members or by offers.

216
00:12:14.087 --> 00:12:19.804
This is a lookup into precomputed tables refreshed every ~1 minute by stream jobs.

217
00:12:20.084 --> 00:12:22.495
Feed assembly (§6.6).

218
00:12:22.775 --> 00:12:27.191
The feed service: takes the serviceable set (~400 restaurants);.

219
00:12:28.541 --> 00:12:47.293
scores each one for this user with a ranking model (cuisine affinity, past orders, price band, rating, ETA, offers, freshness);. mixes in ads: sponsored slots labelled and auctioned (Ch 43);. builds widgets such as Order again, Top picks for you, Under 30 mins and offer carousels.

220
00:12:47.573 --> 00:12:48.490
Response.

221
00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:51.565
The feed is returned in ~200 ms.

222
00:12:51.845 --> 00:12:53.679
Images come from the CDN.

223
00:12:53.959 --> 00:12:55.282
Impressions are logged.

224
00:12:55.562 --> 00:12:56.522
Rain mode.

225
00:12:56.802 --> 00:12:59.021
If it's raining in this zone, the feed may:

226
00:13:00.371 --> 00:13:04.381
show fewer far restaurants;. show higher fees and ETAs;. show a banner:

227
00:13:04.661 --> 00:13:07.648
High demand due to rain, delivery may take longer.

228
00:13:08.998 --> 00:13:10.790
Journey 2: placing an order.

229
00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:11.817
Menu.

230
00:13:12.097 --> 00:13:17.643
Sameer opens a biryani restaurant and adds Chicken Biryani (full) with extra raita.

231
00:13:17.923 --> 00:13:23.427
The menu service shows availability, which the restaurant can toggle out of stock in real time.

232
00:13:23.707 --> 00:13:24.433
Cart.

233
00:13:24.713 --> 00:13:34.569
The cart service prices the order (§5.4): items and add-ons;. packaging charges;. delivery fee (distance and surge);. platform fee;.

234
00:13:35.919 --> 00:13:54.308
GST: restaurant services through e-commerce operators at 5%, with the platform collecting and paying under Section 9(5) (VERIFY);. offers (restaurant-funded 50% off up to 100 rupees, platform coupons, membership);. final total and ETA promise.

235
00:13:54.588 --> 00:13:55.484
Checkout.

236
00:13:55.764 --> 00:13:58.729
Address confirmation; a pin drop helps the rider.

237
00:14:00.079 --> 00:14:04.815
Payment by UPI: the payment intent is created with an idempotency key.

238
00:14:05.095 --> 00:14:07.954
On success the order is created in the PLACED state.

239
00:14:08.234 --> 00:14:15.231
With COD, the order is created directly, with risk checks such as COD limits for new users or risky addresses.

240
00:14:15.511 --> 00:14:16.450
Order event.

241
00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:18.671
OrderPlaced is published to Kafka.

242
00:14:18.951 --> 00:14:21.725
Consumers react: restaurant notification;.

243
00:14:22.005 --> 00:14:25.439
ETA service (initial prediction);. dispatch (pre-planning);.

244
00:14:26.789 --> 00:14:27.749
analytics.

245
00:14:28.029 --> 00:14:29.203
Customer screen.

246
00:14:29.483 --> 00:14:32.064
Waiting for restaurant to accept with the ETA.

247
00:14:33.414 --> 00:14:35.953
Journey 3: restaurant acceptance and prep.

248
00:14:36.233 --> 00:14:37.321
Notification.

249
00:14:37.601 --> 00:14:42.763
The restaurant's partner app (a tablet at the counter) rings loudly with the order details.

250
00:14:43.043 --> 00:14:44.046
Acceptance.

251
00:14:44.326 --> 00:14:52.219
The staff accept within ~1 to 3 minutes and the app suggests a prep time, for example, 18 minutes, which they can adjust.

252
00:14:52.499 --> 00:14:55.593
Auto-accept is available for reliable restaurants.

253
00:14:55.873 --> 00:14:59.563
Timeout or rejection triggers an attempt to call the restaurant.

254
00:14:59.843 --> 00:15:07.481
If that fails, the order is cancelled with a full refund, and the restaurant is penalised in ranking or reliability scores.

255
00:15:08.831 --> 00:15:11.497
Prep-time prediction (§6.2).

256
00:15:11.777 --> 00:15:23.831
The platform's model predicts the actual food-ready time from: the restaurant's history;. the items;. current kitchen load (open orders at that restaurant);. time of day;. the staff's entered estimate.

257
00:15:25.181 --> 00:15:29.255
Restaurants often under- or over-estimate, so the model corrects for it.

258
00:15:29.535 --> 00:15:30.453
Mark ready.

259
00:15:30.733 --> 00:15:32.866
The restaurant taps Food ready when packed.

260
00:15:33.146 --> 00:15:40.037
This signal is noisy (some mark ready early to look good), so models also use rider wait times to calibrate.

261
00:15:40.317 --> 00:15:41.554
Item out of stock.

262
00:15:41.834 --> 00:15:44.223
The restaurant marks an item unavailable.

263
00:15:44.503 --> 00:15:50.349
The customer gets a prompt to choose a substitute or accept a partial refund, with a timeout default.

264
00:15:51.699 --> 00:15:53.725
Journey 4: dispatch and pickup.

265
00:15:54.005 --> 00:15:54.795
Timing.

266
00:15:55.075 --> 00:16:08.835
Dispatch wants the rider to arrive at the restaurant just as the food is ready: if the rider is early, they wait, which wastes rider time and capacity;. if they are late, the food waits and gets cold, and the ETA is missed.

267
00:16:09.115 --> 00:16:14.704
Dispatch therefore plans an assignment around (predicted ready time minus rider travel time).

268
00:16:14.984 --> 00:16:17.715
Batch optimisation (§6.4).

269
00:16:17.995 --> 00:16:21.408
Every ~10 to 20 s in each zone, the dispatcher:

270
00:16:22.758 --> 00:16:41.894
collects unassigned orders and available riders: idle riders, and riders finishing a delivery nearby (chained);. computes costs (travel time to restaurant, wait at restaurant, delay to the customer versus promise, rider fairness);. solves an assignment problem (Hungarian or min-cost matching,

271
00:16:42.174 --> 00:16:53.139
or heuristics);. considers batching: two orders from the same or nearby restaurants going in the same direction can share a rider if neither customer's ETA suffers too much.

272
00:16:54.489 --> 00:16:55.641
Offer to the rider.

273
00:16:55.921 --> 00:17:01.169
The rider app shows the order (restaurant, distance, estimated earnings) and the rider accepts.

274
00:17:01.449 --> 00:17:06.036
Rejections or timeouts send the order back to the pool, with a higher priority.

275
00:17:06.316 --> 00:17:07.063
Pickup.

276
00:17:07.343 --> 00:17:12.655
The rider navigates to the restaurant and is geofenced on arrival (arrived at restaurant).

277
00:17:14.005 --> 00:17:20.191
Food is handed over, the rider confirms the order ID and items, and the status becomes PICKEDUP.

278
00:17:20.471 --> 00:17:23.266
Rider wait time at the restaurant is recorded.

279
00:17:23.546 --> 00:17:25.807
It is a key signal for prep-time models.

280
00:17:26.087 --> 00:17:26.962
Delivery.

281
00:17:27.242 --> 00:17:34.047
The rider navigates to the customer, and the live location streams to the customer's map (§5.6).

282
00:17:35.397 --> 00:17:44.613
The ETA is recomputed continuously from remaining route time plus a last-mile estimate (building access, finding the flat, apartment security).

283
00:17:44.893 --> 00:17:47.389
Contact uses masked phone calls or chat.

284
00:17:47.669 --> 00:17:50.955
Delivered: confirmed by geofence plus the rider tap.

285
00:17:51.235 --> 00:17:54.541
High-value orders may require a delivery OTP.

286
00:17:54.821 --> 00:17:55.888
After delivery.

287
00:17:56.168 --> 00:17:58.045
Rating prompts go to the customer.

288
00:17:59.395 --> 00:18:01.145
Rider earnings are updated.

289
00:18:01.425 --> 00:18:06.011
The rider becomes available, often already pre-assigned to a next order nearby.

290
00:18:07.361 --> 00:18:10.455
Journey 5: the rain spike at 8:30 pm.

291
00:18:10.735 --> 00:18:11.652
Detection.

292
00:18:11.932 --> 00:18:16.135
Order rates in Bengaluru zones jump 60% in 10 minutes.

293
00:18:16.415 --> 00:18:20.191
Rider supply drops, because some riders log off in heavy rain.

294
00:18:20.471 --> 00:18:21.836
Travel times increase.

295
00:18:22.116 --> 00:18:26.809
Zone stress, measured as open orders per available rider, crosses thresholds.

296
00:18:27.089 --> 00:18:31.633
Automatic levers, per zone, in a graduated order (§6.5):

297
00:18:31.913 --> 00:18:36.287
ETA inflation: show honest, longer ETAs so promises aren't broken.

298
00:18:36.567 --> 00:18:40.833
Rain or surge fee: this funds rider incentives and tempers demand.

299
00:18:42.183 --> 00:18:43.399
Rider incentives:

300
00:18:43.679 --> 00:18:49.077
Rain bonus 30 rupees per order for the next 2 hours pushed to offline riders nearby.

301
00:18:49.357 --> 00:18:53.581
Serviceability radius shrink: restaurants far from customers are hidden.

302
00:18:53.861 --> 00:18:57.615
Batching more aggressively: two orders per rider where routes align.

303
00:18:57.895 --> 00:19:01.586
Throttle: pause orders from restaurants with long prep backlogs.

304
00:19:01.866 --> 00:19:05.749
In extreme cases, show Not accepting orders in your area right now.

305
00:19:06.029 --> 00:19:06.903
Monitoring.

306
00:19:07.183 --> 00:19:11.173
Ops dashboards show zone stress, ETA breaches and rider supply.

307
00:19:11.453 --> 00:19:13.970
City ops can override the levers manually.

308
00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:16.237
Recovery.

309
00:19:16.517 --> 00:19:21.829
As the rain stops, the levers relax gradually (hysteresis) to avoid oscillation.

310
00:19:23.179 --> 00:19:25.526
Journey 6: a missing item complaint.

311
00:19:25.806 --> 00:19:26.681
Complaint.

312
00:19:26.961 --> 00:19:31.249
Sameer reports raita missing in the app's help section, with a photo option.

313
00:19:31.529 --> 00:19:32.979
Self-serve resolution.

314
00:19:33.259 --> 00:19:46.827
A rule and risk engine decides: instant refund for low-value items when the customer is trustworthy (low complaint history);. escalation to an agent when the risk is high (frequent complaints, high-value claims).

315
00:19:47.107 --> 00:19:47.961
Refund.

316
00:19:48.241 --> 00:19:55.195
Issued to the source (UPI or card) or as platform credits, depending on the customer's choice and policy.

317
00:19:56.545 --> 00:19:57.569
Attribution.

318
00:19:57.849 --> 00:20:04.527
The cost is attributed to the restaurant (packing error) or the rider (tampering), based on evidence and patterns.

319
00:20:04.807 --> 00:20:09.820
It affects the restaurant's settlement through deductions, per contract, or its quality score.

320
00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:16.585
Abuse detection (§6.8): repeat complainers, linked accounts and photo reuse are flagged.

321
00:20:17.935 --> 00:20:20.901
Journey 7: quick commerce (10-minute groceries).

322
00:20:21.181 --> 00:20:22.247
Dark stores.

323
00:20:22.527 --> 00:20:28.991
Small warehouses within ~2 to 3 km of customers hold ~5 to 8k SKUs.

324
00:20:29.271 --> 00:20:31.213
Each store serves a tight polygon.

325
00:20:31.493 --> 00:20:32.218
Order.

326
00:20:32.498 --> 00:20:34.994
The customer orders milk, bread and chips.

327
00:20:35.274 --> 00:20:42.143
Serviceability shows a 10 mins ETA, available only if the store has stock and pickers and riders are available.

328
00:20:42.423 --> 00:20:43.234
Picking.

329
00:20:43.514 --> 00:20:45.775
The order drops to the store's picking system.

330
00:20:46.055 --> 00:20:52.733
The pick route is optimised by slotting, since fast movers sit near the packing station (§6.9).

331
00:20:54.083 --> 00:20:57.347
Picking takes ~2 minutes and packing ~1 minute.

332
00:20:57.627 --> 00:20:58.544
Dispatch.

333
00:20:58.824 --> 00:21:01.704
Riders wait at the store, so assignment is instant.

334
00:21:01.984 --> 00:21:05.547
Delivery takes ~5 to 7 minutes over short distances.

335
00:21:05.827 --> 00:21:06.765
Inventory.

336
00:21:07.045 --> 00:21:08.773
Real-time stock per store.

337
00:21:09.053 --> 00:21:15.667
Demand forecasting per SKU per store per hour drives replenishment from the mother warehouse (I12).

338
00:21:15.947 --> 00:21:18.485
Stock-outs hide items from the app immediately.

339
00:21:19.835 --> 00:21:20.795
Part 5.

340
00:21:21.075 --> 00:21:22.611
Core Subsystems in Depth.

341
00:21:23.861 --> 00:21:26.229
Geo model: zones and hexagons.

342
00:21:26.509 --> 00:21:36.280
Hexagons: the H3 grid (open-sourced by Uber) at a resolution where each cell is about 0.1 to 0.7 km².

343
00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:40.763
It is used for: demand and supply counts;. serviceability tables;.

344
00:21:41.043 --> 00:21:44.349
ETA features (travel time between hexes);. surge.

345
00:21:44.629 --> 00:21:51.904
Zones: operational areas (polygons) made of hexes, each with its own rider fleet, ops team and lever settings.

346
00:21:53.254 --> 00:22:01.745
Travel time matrix: hex-to-hex travel times by time of day, learned from rider GPS traces, plus live traffic adjustments.

347
00:22:03.095 --> 00:22:04.908
Serviceability and zone state.

348
00:22:05.188 --> 00:22:21.807
Inputs, refreshed every 30 to 60 s by stream jobs: restaurant status (open, accepting, current prep backlog);. zone stress (orders per available rider, rider wait times, ETA breach rate);. rain flags and weather;. lever settings (radius, fees).

349
00:22:22.087 --> 00:22:27.591
Precomputed table: per (restaurant, customer hexagon) it holds: serviceable or not;.

350
00:22:28.941 --> 00:22:31.373
ETA band;. delivery fee;. distance.

351
00:22:31.653 --> 00:22:45.541
Restaurants times nearby hexes is a manageable size, for example, 250k times ~300 hexes about 75M entries, held in Redis or an in-memory KV keyed by hex.

352
00:22:45.821 --> 00:22:53.479
Consistency: the cart re-checks serviceability and fees at checkout, so the customer always sees a fresh promise before paying.

353
00:22:54.829 --> 00:22:56.173
Catalogue and menus.

354
00:22:56.453 --> 00:23:12.773
Structure: restaurant, then menu categories, then items;. items carry variants (half or full), add-on groups (min or max choices), dietary tags, images, prices and tax category;. availability windows (breakfast menu) and out-of-stock flags.

355
00:23:13.053 --> 00:23:20.691
Updates: restaurants edit through the partner app or POS integrations (some POS systems push menus and stock).

356
00:23:20.971 --> 00:23:22.357
Changes are versioned.

357
00:23:22.637 --> 00:23:25.155
The cart validates against the current version.

358
00:23:26.505 --> 00:23:38.387
Search index: dishes and restaurants are indexed with synonyms (biriyani = biryani), transliteration (Hindi or Kannada typed in English) and spell correction (Ch 42).

359
00:23:39.737 --> 00:23:40.889
Cart and pricing.

360
00:23:41.169 --> 00:23:50.449
Components: item subtotal;. packaging;. delivery fee (distance-based, plus surge, minus member waiver);. small-cart fee;. platform fee;.

361
00:23:50.729 --> 00:23:52.479
GST (per tax rules);.

362
00:23:53.829 --> 00:24:01.167
discounts with funding attribution (restaurant-funded versus platform-funded);. tip (optional, passed fully to the rider).

363
00:24:01.447 --> 00:24:08.146
Offer engine (Ch 49): eligibility rules, stacking rules, budget caps and per-user limits.

364
00:24:08.426 --> 00:24:14.293
Price snapshot: the final breakdown is stored with the order for invoices, settlements and refunds.

365
00:24:15.643 --> 00:24:16.773
Order management.

366
00:24:17.053 --> 00:24:18.077
State machine:

367
00:24:18.357 --> 00:24:24.416
PLACED, then ACCEPTED, then (RIDERASSIGNED), then FOODREADY, then PICKEDUP, then DELIVERED;. with branches:

368
00:24:24.696 --> 00:24:31.608
REJECTED, CANCELLEDBYCUSTOMER or RESTAURANT or PLATFORM, UNDELIVERED (customer not reachable), and so on.

369
00:24:31.888 --> 00:24:34.491
Event-sourced: each transition is an event.

370
00:24:34.771 --> 00:24:40.275
Consumers include notifications, tracking, ETA, settlements, support and analytics.

371
00:24:41.625 --> 00:24:47.299
Timers: acceptance timeout, food-ready overdue, rider-not-assigned alerts, delivery overdue.

372
00:24:47.579 --> 00:24:51.654
These are scheduled timers (Ch 50) that trigger escalations.

373
00:24:51.934 --> 00:25:01.321
Idempotency: client retries, payment callbacks and rider app retries over flaky networks all carry idempotency keys and state-transition guards.

374
00:25:02.671 --> 00:25:04.121
Location and tracking.

375
00:25:04.401 --> 00:25:20.508
Ingest: rider apps send GPS every ~4 s while on a trip, less often when idle;. batches are uploaded when the network is poor;. the server applies map matching (snap to road), filters outliers and detects spoofing (I05).

376
00:25:20.788 --> 00:25:31.113
Live geo index: an in-memory index (H3 cells or a geohash) of available riders, queried by dispatch for riders within X minutes of the restaurant.

377
00:25:31.393 --> 00:25:32.588
Customer tracking:

378
00:25:33.938 --> 00:25:44.946
the rider's position is pushed through the realtime gateway every few seconds;. the client animates smoothly between points;. for privacy, it is shown only during the active order.

379
00:25:46.296 --> 00:25:48.067
Fleet, shifts and incentives.

380
00:25:48.347 --> 00:26:02.875
Riders: log in to slots or shifts (some platforms use slot booking for guaranteed earnings windows);. get zone assignments;. get incentive programmes (orders completed in the peak window, rain bonuses, weekly targets).

381
00:26:03.155 --> 00:26:08.445
Earnings: base pay per order plus distance pay, wait-time pay, incentives and tips.

382
00:26:08.725 --> 00:26:10.411
Earnings are transparent in the app.

383
00:26:11.761 --> 00:26:25.158
Supply planning: forecast demand per zone per 30 minutes;. compute the required riders from expected orders times time per order ÷ batching factor;. recruit and incentivise to fill gaps.

384
00:26:26.508 --> 00:26:27.404
Settlements.

385
00:26:27.684 --> 00:26:37.220
Restaurants: order value minus commission, payment gateway fees, restaurant-funded discounts and penalties, minus TDS or TCS (VERIFY).

386
00:26:37.500 --> 00:26:41.873
Paid on a weekly cycle or faster (some platforms offer daily payouts).

387
00:26:42.153 --> 00:26:44.393
Riders: daily or weekly payouts.

388
00:26:44.673 --> 00:26:50.881
COD cash collected by riders is reconciled against their earnings: deposit or deduction.

389
00:26:51.161 --> 00:26:58.628
Ledger: double-entry, per order and per party, with reconciliation against payment gateway reports (I03).

390
00:26:59.978 --> 00:27:00.917
Part 6.

391
00:27:01.197 --> 00:27:02.157
Algorithms.

392
00:27:03.407 --> 00:27:04.751
ETA decomposition.

393
00:27:05.031 --> 00:27:14.887
Total ETA = taccept + tdispatch + max(tridertorestaurant, tprepremaining) + tpickup + ttraveltocustomer + tlastmile.

394
00:27:15.167 --> 00:27:17.769
Each term has its own model with its own features.

395
00:27:18.049 --> 00:27:34.199
For example: ttravel: the hex-to-hex travel time matrix, plus route distance, time of day, weather and live zone speed;. tlastmile: learned per building or society from history (gated communities, high-rises);. tprep: §6.2.

396
00:27:35.549 --> 00:27:39.410
Prediction targets: a distribution, via quantile regression.

397
00:27:39.690 --> 00:27:47.434
The promise shown to the customer is a conservative quantile (for example, P80), while dispatch planning uses the median.

398
00:27:47.714 --> 00:27:55.821
Continuous updates: after each event (accept, rider assigned, picked up), recompute with the actual times known so far.

399
00:27:56.101 --> 00:27:58.853
Tell the customer if the ETA moves materially.

400
00:27:59.133 --> 00:28:00.413
Public descriptions:

401
00:28:00.693 --> 00:28:09.077
DoorDash and Swiggy have published about ETA models using gradient boosting and deep learning, with multi-stage predictions (VERIFY).

402
00:28:10.427 --> 00:28:11.728
Prep-time prediction.

403
00:28:12.008 --> 00:28:29.459
Features: restaurant;. number and type of items (biryani is quick to serve; pizza needs baking time);. open orders at the restaurant (kitchen load);. time of day and day of week;. the staff-entered prep estimate;. the restaurant's historical bias between entered and actual times.

404
00:28:30.809 --> 00:28:45.486
Labels: true food-ready time is not directly observed, because the mark ready tap is noisy;. use rider arrival and pickup times: if the rider waited, food was ready later than arrival; if the food waited, it was ready earlier,

405
00:28:45.766 --> 00:28:54.534
estimated from the ready tap or pickup time;. this is a censored-data problem, handled with survival-style or censored-regression techniques.

406
00:28:55.884 --> 00:29:00.855
Use: time the rider assignment so arrival about ready time (§6.4).

407
00:29:01.135 --> 00:29:04.164
Dynamic updates come from the restaurant's real-time load.

408
00:29:05.514 --> 00:29:07.029
Dispatch cost function.

409
00:29:07.309 --> 00:29:19.298
For an assignment of rider r to order o, the cost combines: rider travel time to the restaurant;. expected rider wait = max(0, readytime minus arrivaltime).

410
00:29:19.578 --> 00:29:26.618
This wastes rider capacity;. expected food wait = max(0, arrivaltime minus readytime).

411
00:29:26.898 --> 00:29:34.898
This cools the food and delays the customer;. customer delay versus promise: the penalty grows steeply after the promised time;.

412
00:29:36.248 --> 00:29:49.325
rider fairness: spread orders across riders, and avoid giving long trips to riders near the end of their shift;. chaining benefit: a rider finishing a nearby delivery is available soon at a known location.

413
00:29:50.675 --> 00:29:52.041
Assignment and batching.

414
00:29:52.321 --> 00:30:14.635
Batch matching: every Δt (10 to 30 s) per zone, build a bipartite graph of orders times riders, including soon-available riders;. edge costs come from §6.3;. solve min-cost bipartite matching with the Hungarian algorithm (O(n³), fine for a few hundred) or auction algorithms and greedy heuristics at bigger sizes.

415
00:30:14.915 --> 00:30:16.067
Delayed assignment:

416
00:30:17.417 --> 00:30:20.447
don't assign immediately if food will take 20 minutes.

417
00:30:20.727 --> 00:30:28.897
Wait until the latest good assign time (ready time minus travel time minus buffer);. this keeps riders free for urgent orders.

418
00:30:29.177 --> 00:30:31.161
It is a rolling-horizon decision.

419
00:30:31.441 --> 00:30:50.577
Batching (two or more orders per rider): candidate pairs share the same or a nearby restaurant, have ready times within a few minutes, and have customers in a similar direction;. evaluate the insertion cost: the extra delay for each order compared with solo delivery, which must stay under thresholds and promises;.

420
00:30:51.927 --> 00:31:00.610
this is a vehicle-routing problem with pickup and delivery and time windows (VRPPDTW) for small sets, solved heuristically.

421
00:31:00.890 --> 00:31:03.087
Rejections: riders can decline.

422
00:31:03.367 --> 00:31:10.919
Model the acceptance probability (distance, pay, rider history) in the cost, so likely-rejected offers aren't made.

423
00:31:11.199 --> 00:31:12.479
Public descriptions:

424
00:31:12.759 --> 00:31:18.818
Swiggy and DoorDash have blogged about batch dispatch optimisation and assignment timing (VERIFY).

425
00:31:20.168 --> 00:31:21.725
Surge and demand shaping.

426
00:31:22.005 --> 00:31:35.765
Zone stress metric: for example, (open unassigned orders + expected orders next 15 minutes) ÷ (available riders + riders becoming free in 15 minutes), alongside ETA breach rates.

427
00:31:36.045 --> 00:31:38.136
Levers, by escalating thresholds:

428
00:31:38.416 --> 00:31:39.589
ETA inflation.

429
00:31:39.869 --> 00:31:41.939
Surge fee (a capped step function).

430
00:31:42.219 --> 00:31:45.440
Rider incentives (push to nearby offline riders).

431
00:31:45.720 --> 00:31:46.829
Radius shrink.

432
00:31:48.179 --> 00:31:49.523
Restaurant throttling.

433
00:31:49.803 --> 00:31:50.806
Order pausing.

434
00:31:51.086 --> 00:31:56.910
Control loop: a PID-like or rule-based controller with hysteresis, so levers don't flap.

435
00:31:57.190 --> 00:32:01.905
Experiment carefully, since surge affects both customer trust and rider earnings.

436
00:32:02.185 --> 00:32:09.673
Rider repositioning: suggest that idle riders move to hexes with predicted demand, using heatmaps in the rider app.

437
00:32:11.023 --> 00:32:12.196
Discovery ranking.

438
00:32:12.476 --> 00:32:17.084
Candidates: serviceable restaurants, plus dish-level candidates for search.

439
00:32:17.364 --> 00:32:20.287
Model: a multi-objective learning-to-rank model.

440
00:32:20.567 --> 00:32:26.220
It predicts P(click), P(order click) and expected satisfaction (a good rating, no complaint).

441
00:32:26.500 --> 00:32:30.639
It is combined with ETA and price fit, plus diversity and freshness.

442
00:32:30.919 --> 00:32:37.767
Features: user cuisine affinity and embeddings;. restaurant quality (rating, complaint rate, acceptance rate);.

443
00:32:38.047 --> 00:32:38.772
ETA;.

444
00:32:40.122 --> 00:32:45.391
offers;. time-of-day fit (breakfast places in the morning);. repeat behaviour (Order again).

445
00:32:45.671 --> 00:32:55.869
Ads blending: sponsored candidates from an auction (bid times predicted CTR times quality), placed in fixed or labelled slots with relevance thresholds.

446
00:32:56.149 --> 00:32:59.114
New restaurants: cold-start exploration slots.

447
00:33:00.464 --> 00:33:02.768
Demand forecasting and supply planning.

448
00:33:03.048 --> 00:33:16.979
Forecast orders per zone per 15 to 30 minutes for the next day and the next hour, using: history and seasonality;. weather forecasts;. holidays and festivals;. events (cricket matches);. marketing campaigns.

449
00:33:18.329 --> 00:33:24.835
Use: rider shift planning, incentive budgets, restaurant prep readiness alerts, and quick-commerce replenishment.

450
00:33:26.185 --> 00:33:27.359
Fraud and abuse.

451
00:33:27.639 --> 00:33:37.409
Customer refund abuse: frequent missing item claims, linked accounts (device, phone, address, payment instrument graph), photo reuse (perceptual hash).

452
00:33:37.689 --> 00:33:41.017
The response is a risk score that gates instant refunds.

453
00:33:41.297 --> 00:33:45.244
COD fraud: fake orders and repeated refusals at the door.

454
00:33:45.524 --> 00:33:48.041
The response is COD limits per risk tier.

455
00:33:48.321 --> 00:33:49.409
Rider fraud:

456
00:33:49.689 --> 00:33:58.073
GPS spoofing (mock locations, impossible jumps), marking delivered without delivering, collusion with customers on claims.

457
00:33:58.353 --> 00:34:01.383
Detect with sensor consistency and pattern models.

458
00:34:02.733 --> 00:34:06.359
Restaurant fraud: fake orders to farm ratings or offers.

459
00:34:06.639 --> 00:34:10.245
Use graph analysis of customers, devices and restaurants.

460
00:34:10.525 --> 00:34:13.810
Promo abuse: multiple accounts for new-user offers.

461
00:34:14.090 --> 00:34:16.629
Use device fingerprinting and account linking.

462
00:34:17.979 --> 00:34:19.557
Quick-commerce algorithms.

463
00:34:19.837 --> 00:34:28.136
Store slotting: place fast-moving SKUs near the packing station, grouped by co-purchase affinity, to minimise pick path length.

464
00:34:28.416 --> 00:34:35.605
Pick routing: a short path through the store (a TSP heuristic on the aisle graph) and batch picking of multiple orders.

465
00:34:35.885 --> 00:34:44.888
Replenishment: a forecast per SKU per store per hour, a safety stock for the service level, and constraints from mother warehouse truck schedules.

466
00:34:45.168 --> 00:34:46.597
Serviceability promise:

467
00:34:46.877 --> 00:34:51.464
10 minutes only if stock is available and the picker and rider queues are short.

468
00:34:51.744 --> 00:34:54.432
Otherwise show a longer promise or hide the store.

