658
00:43:39.206 --> 00:43:40.145
Part 12.

659
00:43:40.425 --> 00:43:41.769
Interview Q and A.

660
00:43:43.019 --> 00:43:43.851
Beginner.

661
00:43:44.131 --> 00:43:45.155
Question 1.

662
00:43:45.435 --> 00:43:47.568
What are the steps of a food delivery order?

663
00:43:47.848 --> 00:43:49.213
Thirty-second answer:

664
00:43:49.493 --> 00:44:07.243
Order placed and paid, restaurant accepts with a prep time, a rider is assigned timed to food readiness, rider picks up, delivers with live tracking, and the order is rated and settled, each step is a state transition event consumed by notifications, tracking, ETA and settlements.

665
00:44:08.593 --> 00:44:09.595
Question 2.

666
00:44:09.875 --> 00:44:12.691
How do you show which restaurants can deliver to me right now?

667
00:44:12.971 --> 00:44:14.337
Thirty-second answer:

668
00:44:14.617 --> 00:44:27.822
Map the customer location to a hexagon and look up a precomputed serviceability table per (restaurant, hex) refreshed every minute from restaurant status, distance, zone stress and fees; re-check at checkout.

669
00:44:29.172 --> 00:44:30.217
Question 3.

670
00:44:30.497 --> 00:44:32.417
How is live rider tracking built?

671
00:44:32.697 --> 00:44:34.063
Thirty-second answer:

672
00:44:34.343 --> 00:44:51.687
The rider app sends GPS every few seconds; the server snaps to roads and filters noise, updates an in-memory geo index and streams positions to the customer through a realtime gateway; the client animates between points; ETA is recomputed as the rider moves.

673
00:44:53.037 --> 00:44:54.103
Question 4.

674
00:44:54.383 --> 00:44:56.773
Why does the delivery fee change in the rain?

675
00:44:57.053 --> 00:44:58.418
Thirty-second answer:

676
00:44:58.698 --> 00:45:09.279
Demand rises and rider supply falls; a surge fee funds rider incentives and tempers demand so ETAs stay honest, part of graduated zone-level levers.

677
00:45:10.629 --> 00:45:11.632
Intermediate.

678
00:45:11.912 --> 00:45:13.021
Question 5.

679
00:45:13.301 --> 00:45:15.093
How would you predict delivery ETA?

680
00:45:15.373 --> 00:45:16.739
Thirty-second answer:

681
00:45:17.019 --> 00:45:37.563
Decompose it into acceptance, dispatch, max(rider-to-restaurant, prep remaining), pickup, travel to customer and last mile; model each with ML (quantile regression) using features like restaurant load, travel time matrices, weather and building history; promise a conservative quantile and update after each event.

682
00:45:38.913 --> 00:45:40.022
Question 6.

683
00:45:40.302 --> 00:45:44.654
How do you predict restaurant prep time when 'food ready' taps are unreliable?

684
00:45:44.934 --> 00:45:46.299
Thirty-second answer:

685
00:45:46.579 --> 00:46:02.259
Use restaurant, items, kitchen load, time of day and staff estimate as features, and derive labels from rider arrival and pickup times treating them as censored observations (rider waited versus food waited); correct each restaurant's bias.

686
00:46:03.609 --> 00:46:04.697
Question 7.

687
00:46:04.977 --> 00:46:06.407
How do you price a cart?

688
00:46:06.687 --> 00:46:08.052
Thirty-second answer:

689
00:46:08.332 --> 00:46:24.481
Item and add-on prices, packaging, distance and surge delivery fee, platform fee, GST per rules, offers with eligibility and stacking rules and funding attribution, optional tip; store a price snapshot with the order for invoices and settlements.

690
00:46:25.831 --> 00:46:26.855
Question 8.

691
00:46:27.135 --> 00:46:31.103
What happens when a restaurant marks an item out of stock after ordering?

692
00:46:31.383 --> 00:46:32.749
Thirty-second answer:

693
00:46:33.029 --> 00:46:44.741
Notify the customer with substitution options or a partial refund with a timeout default, update the order and price snapshot, adjust the settlement, and track restaurant reliability.

694
00:46:46.091 --> 00:46:47.157
Question 9.

695
00:46:47.437 --> 00:46:50.957
How do you handle missing-item complaints without being exploited?

696
00:46:51.237 --> 00:46:52.603
Thirty-second answer:

697
00:46:52.883 --> 00:47:07.667
Self-serve refunds gated by a risk score built from complaint history, account linking graphs, order value and photo checks; instant for low-risk, agent review for high-risk; attribute costs to restaurant or rider by evidence.

698
00:47:09.017 --> 00:47:09.785
Senior.

699
00:47:10.065 --> 00:47:11.110
Question 10.

700
00:47:11.390 --> 00:47:13.374
Design dispatch for food delivery.

701
00:47:13.654 --> 00:47:15.019
Thirty-second answer:

702
00:47:15.299 --> 00:47:35.011
Per zone every 10 to 30 s, collect unassigned orders and available or soon-available riders, compute costs from travel, rider wait, food wait, promise delay, fairness and acceptance probability, delay assignment until near food-ready, solve min-cost matching, evaluate batching via insertion cost,

703
00:47:35.291 --> 00:47:39.686
offer to riders and re-pool on rejection; fall back to greedy under load.

704
00:47:41.036 --> 00:47:42.167
Question 11.

705
00:47:42.447 --> 00:47:44.857
How do you manage a demand spike when it rains?

706
00:47:45.137 --> 00:47:46.503
Thirty-second answer:

707
00:47:46.783 --> 00:48:00.564
Measure zone stress and apply graduated levers, honest ETAs, surge fees, rider incentives, radius shrink, more batching, restaurant throttling, pausing, with hysteresis and ops overrides; recover gradually.

708
00:48:01.914 --> 00:48:03.002
Question 12.

709
00:48:03.282 --> 00:48:05.927
How do you test changes to dispatch or surge?

710
00:48:06.207 --> 00:48:07.573
Thirty-second answer:

711
00:48:07.853 --> 00:48:24.685
Marketplace interventions interfere across users, so use switchback experiments, alternate treatment and control by zone and time window, plus simulation on historical data before launch, with metrics on ETA accuracy, rider utilisation, cost and conversion.

712
00:48:26.035 --> 00:48:27.315
Question 13.

713
00:48:27.595 --> 00:48:29.856
How would you rank restaurants in the home feed?

714
00:48:30.136 --> 00:48:31.501
Thirty-second answer:

715
00:48:31.781 --> 00:48:48.187
Multi-objective learning-to-rank predicting click, order and satisfaction, combined with ETA, price fit, offers, quality and time-of-day fit, user embeddings and repeat behaviour, plus labelled ads from an auction, diversity and cold-start exploration.

716
00:48:49.537 --> 00:48:50.731
Question 14.

717
00:48:51.011 --> 00:48:53.486
How do you detect rider GPS spoofing?

718
00:48:53.766 --> 00:48:55.131
Thirty-second answer:

719
00:48:55.411 --> 00:49:09.449
Check for mock-location flags, impossible speeds and jumps, mismatch between GPS and network or sensor data, deliveries marked without approaching the customer, and patterns across riders; apply holds and reviews.

720
00:49:10.799 --> 00:49:11.695
Architect.

721
00:49:11.975 --> 00:49:13.169
Question 15.

722
00:49:13.449 --> 00:49:17.289
Design a food delivery platform for 3 million orders a day.

723
00:49:17.569 --> 00:49:18.935
Thirty-second answer:

724
00:49:19.215 --> 00:49:41.337
Hex and zone geo model; precomputed serviceability; personalised feed and search; cart and pricing with offers; event-sourced order management; prep and ETA models; batch dispatch with delayed assignment and batching; live tracking; graduated peak levers; ledgers for settlements; Kafka, Flink features and a lakehouse with switchback experiments.

725
00:49:42.687 --> 00:49:43.882
Question 16.

726
00:49:44.162 --> 00:49:47.426
How do you improve unit economics without hurting customers?

727
00:49:47.706 --> 00:49:49.071
Thirty-second answer:

728
00:49:49.351 --> 00:50:06.994
Increase rider utilisation through better prep prediction and delayed assignment, batching within delay thresholds, demand shaping, reducing refunds via quality and fraud controls, optimising offer spend with targeting and budgets, and measuring contribution margin per order by zone.

729
00:50:08.344 --> 00:50:09.688
Question 17.

730
00:50:09.968 --> 00:50:12.336
What real-time data platform would you build?

731
00:50:12.616 --> 00:50:13.981
Thirty-second answer:

732
00:50:14.261 --> 00:50:33.440
Kafka for all app and order events, Flink for real-time features (restaurant load, zone stress, rider waits) into an online feature store and serviceability tables, a lakehouse for training and analytics, model serving for ETA, dispatch and ranking, and an experimentation platform supporting switchbacks.

733
00:50:34.790 --> 00:50:35.963
Question 18.

734
00:50:36.243 --> 00:50:39.059
How is quick commerce different from restaurant delivery?

735
00:50:39.339 --> 00:50:40.705
Thirty-second answer:

736
00:50:40.985 --> 00:50:58.670
The platform controls the supply node (dark store), so prep becomes picking with slotting and pick routing, inventory and replenishment forecasting become core, riders wait at stores for instant assignment, distances are short, and the 10-minute promise depends on stock and queue checks.

737
00:51:00.020 --> 00:51:01.257
Question 19.

738
00:51:01.537 --> 00:51:04.951
How would you design for gig worker regulations and fairness?

739
00:51:05.231 --> 00:51:06.596
Thirty-second answer:

740
00:51:06.876 --> 00:51:25.756
Transparent earnings breakdowns, explainable assignment and incentive rules, grievance workflows, records of working hours and earnings for welfare contributions, safety features and insurance, no penalties that encourage unsafe driving, and auditability of algorithms where laws require (VERIFY).

741
00:51:27.106 --> 00:51:28.215
Question 20.

742
00:51:28.495 --> 00:51:30.501
Compare food delivery with ride-hailing.

743
00:51:30.781 --> 00:51:32.146
Thirty-second answer:

744
00:51:32.426 --> 00:51:53.247
Both match moving supply with demand in real time using geo indexes, ETAs and surge; food delivery adds a third party (restaurant) with uncertain prep time, perishable goods, batching across orders and timing assignments to readiness, while ride-hailing matches people directly with pickup immediacy and pooling constraints.

745
00:51:54.597 --> 00:51:55.643
Part 13.

746
00:51:55.923 --> 00:51:56.691
Summary.

747
00:51:57.941 --> 00:52:02.954
Food delivery is a three-sided, real-time marketplace with perishable goods.

748
00:52:03.234 --> 00:52:08.653
Every decision balances customer speed, rider utilisation and restaurant efficiency.

749
00:52:08.933 --> 00:52:14.415
Hexagons and zones organise the city for serviceability, ETAs, surge and dispatch.

750
00:52:14.695 --> 00:52:18.045
Serviceability is precomputed and re-checked at checkout.

751
00:52:18.325 --> 00:52:20.351
ETA is decomposed into parts.

752
00:52:20.631 --> 00:52:23.383
Prep-time models learn from rider wait signals.

753
00:52:23.663 --> 00:52:29.039
Dispatch delays assignment until near food-ready and batches orders within delay limits.

754
00:52:30.389 --> 00:52:34.101
Peak handling uses graduated zone levers with hysteresis.

755
00:52:34.381 --> 00:52:37.859
Marketplace changes are tested with switchback experiments.

756
00:52:38.139 --> 00:52:41.701
Settlements, refunds and fraud need ledgers and risk scoring.

757
00:52:41.981 --> 00:52:45.693
Quick commerce adds dark-store picking, slotting and replenishment.

